Classic science and war Minutes
Wilder Penfield (burnt toast)
Ask: During Mrs. Gold’s brain surgery, what everyday smell tells Dr. Penfield he has found the source of her seizures?

Halifax Explosion
Ask: In Vince Coleman’s final telegraph message, what kind of ship does he warn about, and how many people are on the incoming train he is trying to save?

Valour Road
Ask: What was Valour Road’s original name, and how many men from that one street received the Victoria Cross in the First World War?

Vimy Ridge
Ask: According to the officers’ plan, how far behind the creeping artillery barrage are the Canadian soldiers supposed to stay as they advance out of the tunnels?

Nursing Sisters
Ask: On what date and at what Canadian hospital in France does the air raid take place, and what happens to Nursing Sister Eden Pringle that night?

Tommy Prince
Ask: In the Italian farmhouse scene, what disguise does Tommy Prince use when he goes out into the field to repair the broken telephone line under enemy observation, and what everyday object is he carrying?

Social justice, Indigenous, and Black history Minutes
Chanie Wenjack
Ask: At the start of the Minute, how far does the narrator say Chanie’s home is from the residential school, and about how many Indigenous children were forced to attend such schools?

Naskumituwin (Treaty 9)
Ask: What does the Cree word “Naskumituwin” mean, and what second Cree idea (“wiichihiiwaawin”) does the narrator say is at the core of the treaty?

Underground Railroad
Ask: According to the narrator, roughly how many enslaved African Americans came secretly to Canada using the Underground Railroad between 1840 and 1860?

Richard Pierpoint
Ask: How old is Richard Pierpoint when he petitions to form an all-Black unit, and what is the name of that unit in the War of 1812?

Viola Desmond
Ask: What is the name of the movie theatre where Viola Desmond refuses to leave the main floor, and what small difference in ticket tax is used as the excuse to charge her?

Tommy Prince (Indigenous rights angle)
Ask: After the wars, how are non‑Indigenous veterans treated differently from Indigenous veterans in terms of land and benefits, according to the commentary connected to the Minute?

“Canadian invention / innovation” Minutes
Basketball (James Naismith)
Ask: In Naismith’s first experimental game, what everyday container is nailed up and later complained about by the janitor because he “needs them back”?

Superman (Joe Shuster)
Ask: What comparison does Joe Shuster finally settle on to describe how fast his superhero is, just before Lois boards the train?

Discovery of Insulin
Ask: What is the name of the teenager who becomes the first patient successfully treated with insulin, and in which Toronto hospital is he shown?

Grey Owl
Ask: What animal is Grey Owl living with and caring for in his rustic cabin, and what is surprising about where he was actually born?

Women in Canadian history Minutes
Jennie Trout
Ask: Jennie Trout becomes the first woman in Canada licensed to practise which profession, and what obstacle does she face when trying to attend medical lectures in Toronto?

Laura Secord
Ask: Roughly how many American soldiers are forced to surrender after Laura Secord delivers her warning, and to which British officer does she insist on being taken?

Agnes Macphail
Ask: In her visit to the prison, what does Agnes Macphail react to that convinces her the Canadian penal system needs major reform? (Students must describe a specific scene or object from the Minute.)

Modern iconic Minutes
Terry Fox
Ask: In the Heritage Minute, how many miles does Terry Fox say he runs each day, and what powerful line does he say about “where the hurting must stop”?

Winnie
Ask: What is the name of the young boy who visits Winnie at the London Zoo, and how does this meeting inspire a famous children’s story?

Jackie Robinson
Ask: Which Canadian city and team is Jackie Robinson playing for in the Minute, and what historic “barrier” is he breaking by taking the field there?